---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PatronageFlowPlate from '../components/PatronageFlowPlate.astro';
import GenerationsPlate from '../components/GenerationsPlate.astro';
import WhyGenerationsPlate from '../components/WhyGenerationsPlate.astro';

const setup = [
	{
		n: '01',
		title: 'Deploy the contract',
		body: 'Connect a wallet, choose where revenue is paid — your wallet or your Treasury — and deploy. One on-chain transaction plus gas. If you want revenue pooling in a treasury, deploy that first: the payee choice is permanent.',
	},
	{
		n: '02',
		title: 'Open a generation',
		body: 'Set a token fee, which is the minimum donation. Add the FanPin artwork, up to 10 MB, and a description saying whatever you want it to say — or nothing. It goes live as your active generation.',
	},
	{
		n: '03',
		title: 'Point your catalog at it',
		body: 'In the Catalog, grant that generation access to an album or a single track: a date it opens, and whether streaming is free or paid for them. Stack groups to stagger a release across cohorts.',
	},
	{
		n: '04',
		title: 'Close it when you are ready',
		body: 'Creating the next generation closes the last one. That cohort is now sealed, permanently and publicly, and can never be joined again.',
	},
];
---

<Page
	slug="fansociety"
	description="FanSociety is fan club infrastructure built on generations rather than scarcity. A one-time donation mints a FanPin that timestamps when a fan found you, and the cohort seals when you open the next generation."
	lede="A novel crowdfunding solution that works the way you'd expect. Unapologetically anti-subscription."
>
	<section class="section">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Think different</p>
			<h2>Step outside the box with us</h2>

			<div class="two">
				<div class="prose">
					<p class="lede">
						Subscriptions aren't a good fit for fandom. The moment you stop paying,
						you're out of the fan club. Your previous patronage is no longer valuable.
						This isn't really how the music world works in the minds of music junkies.
						Discovering new music is the real social currency.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						FanSociety replaces the recurring charge with a one-time donation that leaves a
						permanent on-chain receipt — a <strong>FanPin</strong>. A FanPin solves two issues at once.
						One, it timestamps when a fan discovered, started following, and supported an artist.
						Two, it consolidates months of subscription patronage into one large gift right now, turning
						a slow drip of funding into a substantial amount to bootstrap an artist's carrer.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<PatronageFlowPlate />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">research and development</p>
			<h2>Finding the perfect fit</h2>
			<p class="lede intro">
				Most patronage models sort fans by how much they paid or how fast they clicked. We wanted one
				that sorts by when someone showed up, at a resolution an artist can actually manage.
			</p>

			<WhyGenerationsPlate />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">The mechanism</p>
			<h2>How a generation works</h2>
			<p class="lede intro">
				Everything about the model is taken from existing social contracts. FanSociety uses a cohorts model that is manageable for artists and fair for fans.
			</p>

			<div class="plate-gap">
				<GenerationsPlate />
			</div>

			<div class="prose wide">
				<p>
					While a generation is open, any number of fans can mint into it at or above your token fee.
					When you create the next one, the previous closes — permanently. Nobody joins Generation 1
					in year four.
				</p>
				<p>
					That single rule is what turns a fan club into a record. Over a career you accumulate
					cohorts, each one stamped with the period it belonged to, and the ordering can never be
					rewritten because it was never stored anywhere you or we could edit.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Access</p>
			<h2>What a FanPin actually opens</h2>

			<div class="two">
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						FanPins can be used by fans to flex on their friends, but more interestingly, artists can give privileged access to content
						based on FanPin holders. This works natively with Station on day one and we see growing number of features that can take
						advantage of the FanPin model.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						FanPins with Station are flexible. Artists can do early releases based on FanPin generations.
						Tracks and albums can even be gated for fan club members only. Station makes this all possible,
						but doesn't tell artists exactly how they can and can't use it.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Setting it up</p>
			<h2>Four steps, from the dashboard</h2>

			<ol class="steps">
				{
					setup.map((s) => (
						<li class="step">
							<span class="n" aria-hidden="true">
								{s.n}
							</span>
							<div>
								<h3>{s.title}</h3>
								<p>{s.body}</p>
							</div>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ol>

			<aside class="callout">
				<span class="stamp">wallet required</span>
				<p>
					The Fan Club is on-chain, so it needs a connected browser wallet holding enough of the
					chain's token to cover the deploy cost and gas. On the current test network those tokens
					are free from a faucet, so you can practise the whole flow — deploy, mint, withdraw —
					without spending anything.
				</p>
			</aside>

			<p class="ask">
				FanSociety is its own protocol, and there's more about it at <a
					href="https://www.fansociety.xyz/"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer">fansociety.xyz</a
				>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>

<style>
	.prose p {
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	.prose p + p {
		margin-top: 1.1rem;
	}

	.prose .lede {
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
	}

	.prose strong {
		color: var(--white);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.prose em {
		font-style: normal;
		color: var(--mint);
	}

	.prose a {
		color: var(--mint);
		text-decoration: none;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--mint-rule);
	}

	.prose a:hover {
		border-bottom-color: var(--mint);
	}

	.wide p {
		max-width: 68ch;
	}

	.intro {
		margin-top: 1.25rem;
	}

	h2 + .two,
	h2 + .prose {
		margin-top: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem);
	}

	.two {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr));
		gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
		align-items: start;
	}

	/* The SVG plates ship with margin: 0, so the page supplies their air. */
	.wrap :global(.pf-plate) {
		margin: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0 0;
	}

	/* GenerationsPlate sets margin:0 inline, which no stylesheet rule can beat,
	   so the air goes on a wrapper instead. Prose sits on both sides of it. */
	.plate-gap {
		margin: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0;
	}

	.wrap :global(.wg-plate) {
		margin: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0 0;
	}

	.steps {
		list-style: none;
		margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
	}

	.step {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(3.5rem, 7rem) 1fr;
		gap: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);
		padding: clamp(1.35rem, 3vw, 2rem) 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
		align-items: start;
	}

	.step:last-child {
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	.step .n {
		font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
		line-height: 0.8;
		letter-spacing: -0.04em;
		color: transparent;
		-webkit-text-stroke: 1px var(--mint);
		user-select: none;
	}

	.step h3 {
		margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
	}

	.step p {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
		max-width: var(--measure);
	}

	.callout {
		display: flex;
		gap: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: flex-start;
		margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
		padding-top: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--mint-rule);
	}

	.callout p {
		flex: 1;
		min-width: 18rem;
		font-size: 0.82rem;
		line-height: 1.75;
		color: var(--gray);
		max-width: 58ch;
	}

	.ask {
		margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--gray);
	}

	.ask a {
		color: var(--mint);
		text-decoration: none;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--mint-rule);
	}

	.ask a:hover {
		border-bottom-color: var(--mint);
	}

	@media (max-width: 40rem) {
		.step {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: 0.6rem;
		}
	}
</style>
